    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm working on a pilot formulation for a low-fat mayonnaise variant, but I'm running into some stability issues with the emulsion in high-acid environments (pH ~3.8). Could you explain the specific molecular role of lecithin in stabilizing the oil-water interface under these acidic conditions, and how the charge on the emulsifier might be affecting the droplet coalescence?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Lecithin (a mixture of phospholipids) forms a protective monolayer at the oil-water interface, where its amphiphilic structure prevents droplet coalescence by physically blocking fusion. In your acidic environment, the headgroups of lecithin, particularly phosphatidylcholine and phosphatidylserine, become protonated, which often reduces the magnitude of the electrostatic repulsive forces between droplets compared to higher pH. This decrease in charge repulsion makes the emulsion more susceptible to coalescence unless sufficient mechanical shear or specific stabilizing agents are used to maintain barrier integrity.",
      "source": "target",
